WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:06.140
400 years ago, Galileo started piecing together
the basic principles of reality—what we

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.890
now call modern science.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:13.620
But the questions he was trying to answer
are as old as humanity itself.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.350
What are we made of?

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:20.090
What are the fundamental building blocks of
the universe from which you, me, the stars,

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:23.000
and everything else is constructed?

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.080
In the centuries since Galileo, thousands
of theories and experiments have peered into

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.160
smaller and smaller distances...
converging on a single picture of the structure

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.390
of matter.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.250
This somewhat daunting-looking formula is
where we end up.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.990
It gives the correct answer to hundreds of
thousands of experiments, in some cases with

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.650
an accuracy that is unprecedented in science.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:51.040
It is, by any measure, the most successful
scientific theory of all time.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.829
And yet for something so extraordinary, we
give it a rubbish name.

00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:58.010
We call it the Standard Model.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.360
I’m David Tong, a theoretical physicist
at the University of Cambridge.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.650
And in this video, we’re going to build
the Standard Model, piece by piece.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:12.940
By the end, I hope you’ll have some intuition
for how all of the parts fit together to create

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:17.810
the fundamental building blocks of our universe.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.280
This is the Standard Model.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.320
It describes how everything in the universe
is made of 12 different types of matter particles,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:30.290
interacting with 3 forces, all bound together
by a rather special particle called the Higgs

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.800
boson.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.580
Before we get going, some caveats.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.250
First...I said “three forces”.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.550
While there are actually four fundamental
forces...

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.220
at play in the universe.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.040
This means that there’s something missing
from this picture.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.741
That would be gravity, the most obvious force
at play in the world around us and yet, in

00:01:50.741 --> 00:01:53.950
some sense, the one we understand least.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.110
We do have a theory of gravity, a very successful
theory.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.500
It was given to us by Albert Einstein and
goes by the name of general relativity.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.110
But there are two good reasons why it’s
not included in the Standard Model.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:11.560
The first is that, at the microscopic level,
the force of gravity is so weak that it barely

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.180
has any effect on a single subatomic particle.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.469
The second is that we don’t really know
how to incorporate general relativity, which

00:02:19.469 --> 00:02:22.389
is a classical theory, into the quantum world.

00:02:22.389 --> 00:02:28.840
We have no idea how to peer into a black hole
where quantum gravity effects are at work.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.359
A second caveat is that the Standard Model
is written in a language known as quantum

00:02:33.359 --> 00:02:35.159
field theory.

00:02:35.159 --> 00:02:39.299
This tells us that matter, at the fundamental
level, is not really made up of particles.

00:02:39.299 --> 00:02:44.419
Instead, it’s made up of fields: fluid-like
objects which are spread throughout all of

00:02:44.419 --> 00:02:46.019
space.

00:02:46.019 --> 00:02:50.840
These fields are engaged in an intricate,
harmonious dance to a music that we call the

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.690
laws of physics.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:57.409
The interactions between the fields produce
the physical world in the form of particles.

00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:05.189
To understand the Standard Model, it’s more
convenient to use the language of particles.

00:03:05.189 --> 00:03:08.920
As we build up the Standard Model, we’re
going to meet lots of particles with an array

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.269
of names that can very quickly become bewildering.

00:03:11.269 --> 00:03:18.590
But there is one classification that is, by
far, the most important: Every particle is

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:24.889
either a fermion, which is a matter particle,
or a boson, which is a force particle.

00:03:24.889 --> 00:03:29.839
The distinction between fermions and bosons
lies in the quantum world.

00:03:29.839 --> 00:03:33.439
Fermions must obey something called the Pauli
exclusion principle.

00:03:33.439 --> 00:03:38.329
Roughly speaking, this means that you can’t
put two fermions on top of each other in space.

00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:42.639
As such, these are the building blocks of
matter.

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:48.079
Bosons, on the other hand, can pile on top
of each other as much as they want because

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:51.059
they’re totally unconstrained by the Pauli
exclusion principle.

00:03:51.059 --> 00:03:56.930
Bosons are the particles that mediate the
forces and we’ll talk about them more later.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:01.569
For now, let’s start by looking at the fermions.

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:06.939
Everything that we’re made of can be reduced
to just three matter particles: an electron

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:11.249
and two species of quarks, known as up and
down quarks.

00:04:11.249 --> 00:04:15.650
The familiar proton and neutron each contain
three quarks.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:22.150
The proton has two up quarks and a down, while
the neutron has two down quarks and an up.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.560
Put protons and neutrons together, and you
have a nucleus.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.220
Add electrons to the mix and you have an atom.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.710
Put a bunch of atoms together and that’s
what you’re made of.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:37.220
All the beauty and complexity that we see
in the world around us can be traced to this

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:45.210
same collection of three particles, rearranged
over and over in different combinations.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:47.139
Next comes the fourth type of matter particle.

00:04:47.139 --> 00:04:51.530
It’s called the neutrino and it’s not
like the others.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:55.129
Neutrinos are extremely light and barely interact
with anything else.

00:04:55.129 --> 00:05:00.669
For example, in the time it took me to say
that, something like 100 trillion neutrinos

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:02.930
passed through your body.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:08.360
Most of them came from the sun, but many of
them have been streaming through the universe

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.180
uninterrupted since the first few seconds
after the Big Bang.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:15.840
So, there we have it: four matter particles.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.590
Three that make up you, me, and everyone we
know, and one very peculiar cosmic ghost flowing

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:22.590
through us all.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.699
Four is a nice simple number.

00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:26.139
But here’s where things start to get weird.

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:27.930
Because Nature didn’t stop there.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:33.169
For reasons that we don’t understand, she
took this collection of four particles and

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:35.979
made two further copies.

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:40.159
This means that there are actually three different
kinds of electron-like particles.

00:05:40.159 --> 00:05:43.759
In addition to the original electron that
we know and love, there are particles called

00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:45.250
the muon and the tau.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:52.159
The muon and the tau behave exactly like an
electron, with one important exception: they’re

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:53.159
heavier.

00:05:53.159 --> 00:05:57.210
The muon is about 200 times heavier than the
electron, the tau almost three and a half

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.759
thousand times heavier.

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.449
The same generational pattern then repeats
for the quarks.

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:08.259
There are two heavier versions of the down
quark, called strange and bottom quarks, and

00:06:08.259 --> 00:06:12.699
two heavier versions of the up quark, called
charm and top.

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:17.449
And then there are also two more neutrinos:
we become slightly unimaginative in our naming

00:06:17.449 --> 00:06:23.860
and call the full collection the electron
neutrino, the muon neutrino, and the tau neutrino.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.900
We don’t see the second and the third generations
of particles in everyday life.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.550
We can create these heavier particles but
they are unstable, which means that they quickly

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:38.330
decay to the first generation of particles
that we’re made of: the electron, up, or

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:39.330
down quarks.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:40.340
Nonetheless, we know they exist.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.919
We can detect them in particle accelerators.

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:48.039
In some cases, we’ve even been able to take
photographs of the tracks they leave behind.

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:53.270
So this is the collection of particles that
makes up our world.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:54.629
Three sets of four.

00:06:54.629 --> 00:06:57.400
Now some of this we understand very well.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.580
In particular, we understand why particles
have to come in a set of four.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:06.180
There is a mathematical consistency condition
in the Standard Model that tells us that you

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:09.279
can’t have one particle without the other
three.

00:07:09.279 --> 00:07:15.360
In contrast, we have no idea why there are
three generations rather than any other number.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.300
That’s a complete mystery.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:24.169
There’s a surprising aspect of mathematical
unity here: all particles are described by

00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:26.900
exactly the same equation.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:31.440
This equation was written down in the 1920s
by the physicist Paul Dirac, originally to

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.069
describe the electron.

00:07:33.069 --> 00:07:36.600
But, as we discovered more and more particles
-- quarks and neutrinos -- we realized that

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.050
they too are described by the Dirac equation,
or variants of it.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:42.880
In fact, we now know enough to be sure that
if we found more matter particles, they too

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:43.880
must be described by the Dirac equation.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:44.880
So, that’s the stuff we’re made of.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.120
But we’re still missing something crucial!

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.699
And that’s the forces.

00:07:49.699 --> 00:07:53.000
Without the forces, the universe would be
boring.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:57.990
All of the particles would wander around the
cosmos like lost souls, never interacting,

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.559
never doing anything interesting.

00:08:00.559 --> 00:08:04.189
There are three fundamental forces in the
Standard Model and these allow us to explain

00:08:04.189 --> 00:08:09.490
what we see around us in the universe: electromagnetism,
the strong force, and the weak force.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:12.729
Each of these forces comes with an associated
particle.

00:08:12.729 --> 00:08:17.080
These particles are bosons, the other half
of our particle family.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:20.779
Bosons are force-carrying particles.

00:08:20.779 --> 00:08:24.860
In one way of viewing things, you can think
of the fermions as constantly swapping bosons

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:30.830
between them, affecting their motion and giving
rise to what we call a force.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:34.630
Let’s begin with the most familiar of these
forces.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:39.010
Electromagnetism is responsible for the chemical
properties of the elements and we’ve harnessed

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:43.099
it to create much of modern technology.

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:46.230
It acts on anything that carries electric
charge.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:51.500
That means that it acts on the electron-type
particles and the quarks, but not on the neutrinos

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.899
because neutrinos are electrically neutral.

00:08:55.899 --> 00:09:00.810
An electron sitting in space will give rise
to an electric field which spreads radially

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:05.470
outwards, and attracts or repels any other
electrically charged particle in its neighborhood.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:10.120
But if you look more closely at that electric
field, you will find that it’s comprised

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:16.100
of a collection of particles called photons.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:22.980
The next is the strongest fundamental force
in nature, aptly named the strong force.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:27.980
This force acts only on quarks and, subsequently,
on particles like protons and neutrons that

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:29.230
are made of quarks.

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:33.710
This is the force that holds together the
nuclei of atoms.

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:40.339
It’s also this force that is responsible
for nuclear fission and gives the energy that

00:09:40.339 --> 00:09:44.810
is released in an atomic explosion.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:49.460
Just as the photon is associated to electromagnetism,
there is a particle associated to the strong

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:50.570
force.

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:54.940
We call it the gluon because it literally
sticks quarks together.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:10:00.750
Similarly, as the electron gives rise to an
electric field, so a quark sitting in space

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:02.709
will give rise to a gluon field.

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:09.480
But something miraculous happens: unlike electromagnetism,
the field doesn’t spread out radially.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:15.610
Instead, the quark produces a thin flux tube,
a string-like object, which can only end when

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:18.740
it finds a different kind of quark.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:22.800
This is what makes the strong force strong.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.910
Because the two quarks are joined by a flux
tube, it takes more and more energy to pull

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.199
them apart.

00:10:29.199 --> 00:10:31.510
This is why we never see quarks on their own.

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:36.130
They’re always bound together by the strong
force inside bigger particles like protons

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:37.130
and
neutrons.

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:38.920
This is a phenomenon known as confinement.

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:43.690
The third and final force is the most intricate
and subtle of all the forces.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:47.160
This is the weak force.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:52.480
Like the strong force, the weak force only
acts on subatomic distances.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:59.570
But rather than bind particles together, the
weak force is all about decay.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:04.029
We just learned that the strong force binds
quarks together to form protons and neutrons,

00:11:04.029 --> 00:11:06.230
creating the atomic nucleus.

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:11.220
The weak force has the astonishing ability
to allow quarks to switch their identity.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:16.079
For example, a down quark can turn into a
up quark, releasing an electron and neutrino

00:11:16.079 --> 00:11:17.709
in the process.

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:20.310
This means that a neutron can morph into a
proton.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:24.620
This process is called radioactive beta decay.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:29.870
In this way, the weak force is responsible
for the nuclear fusion reactions that power

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:35.050
the sun and produce the energy required for
life on Earth.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:39.459
Finally, the weak force is also the reason
that heavier matter particles, like the muon

00:11:39.459 --> 00:11:42.980
and the strange quark, quickly decay into
the three lighter and more stable fermions

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:51.050
that make up matter as we know it.

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:59.779
The weak force is the only one of the three
forces to act on all the particles.

00:11:59.779 --> 00:12:02.050
In particular, it’s the only force that
neutrinos feel.

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:04.920
There are particles associated to the weak
force and we call these the W and Z bosons.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:09.690
It’s finally time for us to meet the last
piece of the jigsaw: the particle that in

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:14.220
many ways ties the whole Standard Model together.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:18.850
This is the Higgs boson.

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:24.029
To explain why the Higgs boson is special,
I should first tell you a striking fact: none

00:12:24.029 --> 00:12:26.650
of the fundamental particles in the world
have a mass.

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:27.650
In fact, it’s worse than that: the equations
of the Standard Model prohibit the particles

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:28.650
from having any mass!

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:29.650
Massless particles, like the photon are obliged
to travel at the speed of light.

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:30.650
So why don’t matter particles fly around,
massless, at the speed of light?

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:31.650
This is where the Higgs boson comes in.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:32.870
Its role is rather dramatic: it endows all
fermions with a mass.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:34.399
The reason for this doesn’t have to do with
the particle itself, but rather the Higgs

00:12:34.399 --> 00:12:35.399
field that permeates the universe.

00:12:35.399 --> 00:12:40.160
I’d love to be able to give you a clear
explanation of why this happens, but sadly

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:45.800
it’s difficult to come up with good analogies
for the Higgs field.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:54.050
A so-so analogy is that you should view the
Higgs field as something like a cosmic molasses,

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:58.111
trapping matter particles as they travel through
space and giving them a mass.

00:12:58.111 --> 00:12:59.111
We’ve known about the effects of the Higgs
field for a long time.

00:12:59.111 --> 00:13:00.111
But experimental confirmation came only in
2012, when the Large Hadron Collider at CERN

00:13:00.111 --> 00:13:01.111
was able to smash protons together at high
enough energies to cause a ripple in the Higgs

00:13:01.111 --> 00:13:03.750
field—a ripple that’s the particle that
we call the Higgs boson.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:10.670
So this is the Standard Model: 12 matter particles,
interacting with 3 forces and a Higgs field.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:15.480
It’s a beautiful picture, the pinnacle of
400 years of science.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:21.860
But it’s clear that this is not the last
word in physics.

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:26.470
Since the discovery of the Higgs boson, physicists
like me feel that in many ways the Standard

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:28.790
Model is too successful.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:32.450
It gives the right answer for pretty much
every experiment that we can do.

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:36.211
Our current hope is that we will eventually
find an experiment that the Standard Model

00:13:36.211 --> 00:13:38.180
gives the wrong answer to.

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:41.240
And there are some hints that this may be
happening.

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:48.360
Because only then can we get some clues about
what lies beyond.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:53.269
One of the open questions about the model
is whether the three fundamental forces are

00:13:53.269 --> 00:13:58.670
actually different, or whether they are a
manifestation of a single all-encompassing

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:00.019
force.

00:14:00.019 --> 00:14:03.800
This is the dream of a Grand Unified Theory.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:09.830
There are some theoretical signs that this
may be the way things work, but no experimental

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:10.830
confirmation.

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:14.319
What Else Is Missing
Of course, we’re also left with the obvious

00:14:14.319 --> 00:14:17.529
force that’s missing: gravity.

00:14:17.529 --> 00:14:21.269
At the beginning of the video, I talked about
the problem of quantum gravity.

00:14:21.269 --> 00:14:26.470
In recent years we’ve discovered gravitational
waves, which are ripples of space and time

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:27.500
itself.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:32.100
And, if we look closely, there are good reasons
to believe that these waves are made out of

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:37.480
quantum particles called gravitons, just like
light waves are made out of photons.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:40.040
But we’re a long way from discovering individual
gravitons experimentally.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:42.949
There are other things missing from the Standard
Model, too.

00:14:42.949 --> 00:14:47.410
It doesn’t include the invisible realm of
dark matter and dark energy which means that

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:53.260
we’re missing an explanation for a whopping
95% of the energy in the universe.

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.019
Dark matter, for example, is almost certainly
made up of additional particles that don’t

00:14:57.019 --> 00:14:58.550
interact with light.

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:02.850
Perhaps these particles have their own forces
and their own messenger bosons.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:05.980
Outro
And there are still more questions about the

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:09.209
Standard Model that we don’t know how to
answer.

00:15:09.209 --> 00:15:14.130
Why is the muon 200 times heavier than the
electron, while the top quark is almost 350,000

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.250
times heavier than the electron?

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.470
Why are the neutrinos a million times lighter?

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:24.959
We have no idea, and no way of predicting
these masses other than by measuring them

00:15:24.959 --> 00:15:26.800
in experiments.

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:31.722
But there are clearly patterns within these
masses which strongly suggest that there is

00:15:31.722 --> 00:15:35.990
some underlying structure just waiting to
be uncovered.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:40.750
The hope is that, with experimental results
pointing the way, together with new theoretical

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:46.600
ideas, we will ultimately be able to reveal
the next layer of reality and understand what

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:49.149
lies beyond the Standard Model.

00:15:49.149 --> 00:15:56.959
Until then, we continue Galileo’s journey,
with the ultimate goal, a theoretical framework

00:15:56.959 --> 00:16:03.209
to explain the universe and everything in
it: a theory of everything.

